> To dynamically construct a formula in Julia, especially when using the `Lasso` package, you can use the `Symbol` and string manipulation functionalities to create the formula string and then convert it to a formula expression using `Meta.parse`. Here's how you can achieve this:
> 
> 1. Construct the formula string programmatically.
> 2. Use the `Meta.parse` function to convert the string into a Julia expression.
> 3. Evaluate the expression as a formula.
> 
> Here's how you might implement this:
> 
> ```julia
> using Lasso
> 
> # Sample data
> lassodf = DataFrame(yy = rand(100), x1 = rand(100), x2 = rand(100), 
>                     x3 = rand(100), x5 = rand(100), x6 = rand(100))
> 
> # Your list of predictor column names, dynamically constructed
> predictor_columns = names(lassodf)
> skip_variable = :x4 # The variable you want to skip
> 
> # Remove the variable to be skipped
> predictors = setdiff(predictor_columns, [skip_variable; :yy])
> 
> # Construct the formula string
> formula_str = "yy ~ " * join(predictors, " + ")
> 
> # Parse to create the formula
> formula_expr = Meta.parse("@formula($formula_str)")
> 
> # Use the constructed formula
> m = fit(LassoModel, eval(formula_expr), lassodf)
> 
> # Check the fitted model
> m
> ```
> 
> ### Explanation
> - **`names(lassodf)`** obtains the list of column names in the data frame.
> - **`setdiff`** removes the specified variable from your list of predictors.
> - **`join`** constructs a single string containing all predictor names separated by `" + "`.
> - **`Meta.parse`** and **`eval`** are used to create and execute the formula expression. 
> 
> This approach allows you to accommodate a dynamically changing number of predictors while excluding specific ones as needed.
